body:
@gyandeeps Not true-- global return is allowed in Node modules and in some other contexts. `espree` has a globalReturn option which the `node` env enables. astexplorer must not be equipped to handle that.

I will say this-- without enabling `node: true` environment, I also get a parsing error in ESLint CLI and online demo. However, that doesn't mean it's always invalid to have a `return` in global scope.

EDIT: I just went to astexplorer.net and I was able to choose espree as my parser and enable globalReturn option in the parser settings. You should give it a try.

@kaicataldo The problem is that the rule throws an exception, which causes the linting process to terminate. This means other rules cannot function. Surely we can agree that that is something worth avoiding at all costs?

This happens because the rule code simply assumes there will always be a token before `return`. That's not always true. I don't see why the rule needs to be aware of whether it's in a Node environment or not-- it just needs to know that there might not always be a previous token.

What triggers this possibility is actually `{ parserOptions: { ecmaFeatures: { globalReturn: true } } }`. This is automatically enabled by the node environment, but it's not the only way it can be turned on.

@kaicataldo Please feel free to look at at PR #5818. I didn't make any sweeping changes and all the other test cases should still work as they had been.

As for a valid example, well... I accidentally found this when trying to run ESLint on a Node.JS repository where some test files had literally been prevented from executing by adding a `return;` statement at the beginning of the file. So something like this...

``` js
/* File header comments */

// Can't run these tests now due to some issue
return;

describe("some description", function () {
    // tests
});
```

That's obviously a terrible use of global return, but I still think the `newline-before-return` rule shouldn't throw in that case.

I don't think you necessarily made a mistake, @kaicataldo. This is a crazy edge case. But since I found the issue, it just seems like we should fix it (at least in a way where the rule no longer throws).
